<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/advanced/environmental-design-light-heat-air">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A building is a climate modifier: it stands in weather for a century and spends energy
    (and money, and carbon) deciding what its inside will be like instead. Environmental
    design is the discipline of getting the climate to do the work first — <strong>sun,
    daylight, wind and thermal mass arranged by geometry</strong> — before any machine is
    specified, because a watt avoided by orientation costs nothing to run for sixty years.
    The syllabus strands meet here: the built–natural dynamic (PC.3's climate-mitigation
    core) and building integration (SC.6's envelope, environmental control and measurable
    performance). Prior tiers gave you the physics; this chapter is the design arithmetic
    that turns it into wall sections, window schedules and overheating checks.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="sun control, daylight, natural ventilation, the envelope" widget="equationStep" config={envDesignCfg} />

  <h2>Sun: orientation and shading geometry</h2>
  <p>
    Solar geometry is fixed astronomy: the sun's noon altitude is 90° − latitude ± 23.4°
    (summer/winter solstice). At 40° latitude, that is ~73° in June and ~27° in December —
    a 46° swing that is a free seasonal switch if the facade is oriented to use it.
    <strong>South-facing</strong> glass (northern hemisphere) sees high summer sun and low
    winter sun: a horizontal overhang admits the second and blocks the first. The sizing
    rule comes from one triangle: an overhang of depth d shades a window of height h when
    d/h = 1/tan(altitude). Against 60° summer sun, d ≈ 0.58·h; that same overhang at 20°
    winter sun covers only tan(20°)·d ≈ 0.21·h of the window — winter light still floods
    the sill. <strong>East and west</strong> glass face low-angle sun no overhang can catch
    (the sun is sideways at the times it strikes), which is why west glazing drives
    overheating complaints and why the professional default is: orient the long facade
    within about 15° of solar south, minimise west glass, and shade what remains with
    vertical fins or external blinds. The payoffs are quantified: a square metre of
    south glazing under 500 W/m² of winter sun with a solar-heat-gain coefficient of 0.6
    delivers ~300 W of free heat — and the same square metre in July is an air-conditioning
    bill. Shading, orientation and glass selection (SHGC tuned by facade direction) are the
    three levers, in descending order of cost-effectiveness.
  </p>

  <h2>Daylight: quantity with a health dividend</h2>
  <p>
    Daylight is designed with the <strong>daylight factor</strong> — indoor illuminance as a
    percentage of simultaneous outdoor illuminance under an overcast sky: DF 2% is dim
    task territory, 5% is pleasant for most work. Rules of thumb set the geometry: a
    window-head-to-floor rule (daylight penetrates roughly 2 to 2.5 times the height of
    the window head — 3 m head ≈ 6–7.5 m of usable depth, which is why deep plan buildings
    need atria or light wells), and the <strong>light shelf</strong> (a horizontal baffle
    reflecting high-angle sun onto the ceiling, pushing the bright zone deeper while
    shading the desk). Glare is the failure mode: high-contrast windows that satisfy the
    meter and blind the occupants — the ubiquitous lowered office blind is a daylight
    design's honest report card. The health evidence has hardened: bright morning light
    entrains circadian rhythm (the sleep lesson), classrooms with better daylight show
    measurable learning gains (the Heschong-Mahone studies found ~20% faster progress),
    and views out reduce stress — so daylight is now specified as a <em>health</em>
    requirement with minimum levels, not an amenity.
  </p>

  <h2>Air: natural ventilation and the IAQ budget</h2>
  <p>
    Moving air without machines uses two engines. <strong>Cross-ventilation</strong>: wind
    pressure on the windward face and suction on the leeward drive flow through the
    building — it needs two openings on (roughly) opposite sides, which is a plan-layout
    decision made on the drawing, not an equipment choice. <strong>Stack effect</strong>:
    warm indoor air is buoyant; a tall atrium or ventilation chimney with high-level
    outlets vents it, drawing replacement air through low inlets, with driving pressure
    proportional to height times temperature difference. <strong>Night purging</strong>
    couples air to mass: open the building overnight and cool outdoor air discharges the
    heat the concrete slab absorbed during the day, recharging it for tomorrow — vernacular
    architecture from Persian windcatchers to Queensland verandas ran on exactly these
    physics. The indoor-air-quality budget runs on the same ledger: each person exhales
    about 0.005 litres of CO₂ per second, so steady-state CO₂ concentration is a live
    readout of fresh-air supply per occupant; keep it under ~1,000 ppm (roughly 8–10
    litres per second per person) and both odour complaints and the cognitive-performance
    declines documented in decision-making studies stay away. The 1970s energy crisis
    taught this the hard way — airtight, under-ventilated buildings produced
    <em>sick building syndrome</em>, and ventilation was relearned as a health quantity.
  </p>

  <h2>Heat: the envelope's arithmetic</h2>
  <p>
    Conduction through the fabric obeys Q = U·A·ΔT, and the <strong>U-value</strong> is
    computed by adding resistances: U = 1/Σ(R), with each layer contributing R =
    thickness/λ (λ = conductivity). Worked example: upgrade a solid brick wall
    (U ≈ 2.0 W/m²K) by adding 150 mm of mineral wool (λ = 0.035 W/mK). The insulation's
    resistance is 0.15/0.035 ≈ 4.3 m²K/W; add internal and external surface resistances
    (~0.17) and the total is ≈ 4.5, giving U ≈ 0.22. On a 20 K winter difference, heat
    loss falls from 40 W/m² to ~4.4 W/m² — <strong>tenfold, from one design decision</strong>,
    which is why the wall section is the most consequential drawing in the building.
    Glazing is the honest caveat: even triple glazing (U ≈ 0.8) loses four times more per
    m² than a good wall, so the window-to-wall ratio is itself a thermal decision.
    <strong>Thermal mass</strong> adds the time dimension: heavyweight materials store heat
    (a concrete slab can absorb a full day's solar gain and return it overnight), damping
    temperature swings — valuable where days are hot and nights cool, useless without the
    night ventilation to discharge it, and it interacts with insulation (insulate
    <em>outside</em> the mass, or the mass is disconnected from the room).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the environmentally designed building is defined by its
    equipment — solar panels, heat pumps, smart controls. The hierarchy runs the other way:
    <strong>form and fabric first</strong> (orientation, shading, insulation, airtightness,
    mass), then efficiency (heat recovery, LED, efficient plant), then renewables to cover
    the remainder. Equipment on an unshaded, west-glazed, poorly insulated building is
    paying a permanent energy bill for geometry that was free to fix.
  </p>

  <h2>Integration and measurable outcomes (SC.6)</h2>
  <p>
    The wall section is where the disciplines collide: structure (the blockwork or frame
    carries floors), thermal (insulation position decides whether mass works and where
    condensation lands), moisture (a vapour-control layer on the warm side, a breather
    membrane outside — get the order wrong and interstitial rot follows the dew point into
    the fabric), fire (cavity barriers, compartmentation), acoustics (mass and
    decoupling), and buildability (can the sequence actually be assembled watertight?).
    Modern performance frameworks make the outcomes measurable: <strong>U-values</strong>
    per element, whole-building <strong>heat-loss coefficient</strong>, airtightness
    (blower-door tested, in air changes per hour — Passivhaus demands 0.6), <strong>energy
    use intensity</strong> in kWh/m²/yr (Passivhaus space-heating target: 15), overheating
    criteria (hours above a comfort threshold, now modelled per room), daylight factors
    and CO₂. The measurable regime changed the profession's argument style: "it feels
    bright and cool" became "DF 3.4% at the desk line, 26 hours above 26 °C, EUI 68" —
    numbers that can be checked in occupation, and increasingly are (post-occupancy
    evaluation routinely finds the <strong>performance gap</strong>: buildings using 2–3×
    their designed energy, usually via airtightness failures, commissioning shortcuts and
    controls nobody tuned).
  </p>
  <p>
    Adaptation and resilience (PC.3's forward half) extend the same arithmetic to a
    changing climate: designs now face <strong>future weather files</strong> (overheating
    assessed against 2050s projections, not just historic data), <strong>passive
    survivability</strong> — how long a building stays habitable in a heatwave or power
    cut (mass, shading and operable windows are resilience equipment), flood-resilient
    assemblies, and embodied-carbon accounting, as operational energy shrinks and the
    construction slice of the budget dominates: a mass-timber or low-clinker-concrete
    choice is now a carbon decision as quantified as any U-value.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Adaptive comfort rewrote the target.</strong> Fanger's static PMV model put
    everyone at 22–24 °C; field studies across the world (de Dear &amp; Brager) showed
    occupants of naturally ventilated buildings are comfortable across a much wider band
    that tracks the outdoor running mean — roughly 20–28 °C, provided they have
    <em>control</em> (openable windows, fans, blinds). The finding is architectural
    dynamite: it legitimises mixed-mode buildings that run passive first and mechanical
    only when needed, and it quantifies agency — the psychology of control shows up as
    degrees of comfort. The current design synthesis is the adaptive thermal comfort
    standard (EN 16798 / ASHRAE 55 adaptive sections), which sets limits that move with
    the weather instead of a fixed thermostat point.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Simulation changed the profession's epistemology.</strong> Daylight
    (Radiance-based), airflow (CFD), energy (dynamic hourly models) and overheating
    simulations run routinely on serious projects — the performance numbers in the
    section above are outputs, not aspirations. The frontier problems are the gap between
    modelled and measured (occupant behaviour — the window someone opens — remains the
    largest unmodelled variable), the integration of embodied and operational carbon into
    one optimisation (lighter structures save structure but may cost operational mass
    benefits), and <strong>dynamic envelope technologies</strong>: electrochromic glazing
    that switches SHGC on demand, phase-change materials that fake mass in lightweight
    construction, and facade systems that act as batteries. Each tries to abolish a
    static trade-off the geometry above has always imposed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How should comfort standards adapt to climate-change
    temperature distributions whose tails no historic dataset contains? Can the
    performance gap be closed by contract structures (measured, not modelled, performance
    in the fee) rather than better modelling? And what is the optimal balance of fabric
    depth versus adaptability — the deep-retrofit question every existing city now faces,
    where the environmental design of the next fifty years is mostly applied to buildings
    that already exist.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Olgyay, <em>Design with Climate</em> (1963); Szokolay, <em>Introduction to Architectural Science</em>; de Dear &amp; Brager, adaptive comfort field studies; Heschong-Mahone Group, <em>Daylighting in Schools</em>; Passivhaus Institute criteria; CIBSE TM52/TM59 overheating assessments; the passive-design lesson of this ladder.</p>
